        "knowledge_point": "Writing a Complex Number in Exponential Form",
        "problem": "Write the complex number $z=-2+6\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places.",
        "worked_solution": "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-3)| \\\\  & ≈1.893.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\theta =1.893$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\sqrt{10}\\,{\\text{e}}^{1.893\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$",
