        "knowledge_point_id": "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b",
        "knowledge_point": "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown",
        "problem": "The $6$th term of a geometric sequence is $3$, and the common ratio is $3.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$907?$",
        "worked_solution": "Since we are given that ${a}_{6}=3$ and  $r=3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{6-1} \\\\ 3 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{3}^{4}} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={3}^{-4}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={3}^{n-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $907.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{n-5} & >907 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(3)}^{n-5} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(907) \\\\ (n-5)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(907) \\\\ n-5 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+5≈11.199\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $907.$",
