      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-39655",
        "knowledge_point_id": "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b",
        "knowledge_point": "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown",
        "problem": "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $5$, and the $6$th term is $40.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$2\\,020?$",
        "worked_solution": "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=5$ and ${a}_{6}=40$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{6-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{40}{5} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =8 \\\\ r & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{3}=5$ and  $r=2$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {2}^{3-1} \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 5 & =4{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{n-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,020.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {2}^{n-3} & >2\\,020 \\\\ {2}^{n-3} & >404 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{2}^{n-3} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(404) \\\\ (n-3)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(404) \\\\ n-3 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}+3≈11.658\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,020.$",
