        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given in Standard Form",
        "problem": "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9}=1.$",
        "worked_solution": "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(0,-2),\\,a=\\sqrt{16}=4,\\,b=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\ny+2=\\pm \\frac{3}{4}x.\n$$",
