        "problem": "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}=1.$",
        "worked_solution": "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{4}=2,\\,b=\\sqrt{36}=6,\\,h=0,\\,k=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-6) & =\\pm (\\frac{2}{6})(x-0) \\\\ y+6 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
