        "knowledge_point_id": "56abbbc0-080d-5586-931f-957bd2b793b9",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola by Completing the Square",
        "problem": "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $16{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-64x-2y+47=0.$",
        "worked_solution": "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-64x-2y+47 & =0 \\\\ (16{x}^{2}-64x)+(-{y}^{2}-2y)+47 & =0 \\\\ 16[{x}^{2}-4x]-[{y}^{2}+2y]+47 & =0 \\\\ 16[(x-2)^{2}-4]-[(y+1)^{2}-1]+47 & =0 \\\\ 16(x-2)^{2}-64-(y+1)^{2}+1+47 & =0 \\\\ 16(x-2)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =64-1-47 \\\\ 16(x-2)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{16(x-2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{16}{16} \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{1}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nh=2,\\,k=-1,\\,a=\\sqrt{1}=1,\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{1}(x-2) \\\\  & =\\pm 4(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
